Interstitial Cystitis (Bladder Pain Syndrome)
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Suprapubic pain worsening with bladder filling, Relief post-micturition, Profound frequency/urgency

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-021
Reason for Visit: Severe, unremitting pelvic pain and debilitating urinary frequency resulting in acute psychological distress and sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an acute, severe exacerbation of chronic pelvic pain with profound urinary urgency reaching over 50 voids per day, completely precluding sleep. Urgent assessment was strictly required to rule out acute bacterial cystitis, urinary retention, or an acute pelvic emergency, and to initiate immediate pain management that could not be delayed until the next available in-hours appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My bladder feels like it is burning constantly, and the pain is so severe I have to urinate every fifteen minutes just to get a moment of relief.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of unremitting suprapubic and pelvic pain extending well beyond six weeks, which acutely escalated 48 hours ago. Their pain severely exacerbates in direct correlation with bladder filling, achieving profound, albeit temporary, relief immediately following micturition. They experience debilitating urinary urgency driven exclusively by pain, not incontinence. Over the past two days, profound urinary frequency has developed, reaching up to 60 voids per 24 hours. Severe nocturia has resulted in acute sleep deprivation. They note dyspareunia and confirm a recent dietary trigger, having consumed large quantities of highly acidic citrus juices. Negative midstream urine cultures have repeatedly ruled out bacterial cystitis in the past.
Functional Status: Ambulation is preserved but entirely restricted to the immediate vicinity of a toilet.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe exhaustion and acute anxiety due to sleep deprivation and unmanaged pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Visibly exhausted, distressed, shifting uncomfortably, and requiring multiple interruptions to urinate during the consultation.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Severe suprapubic tenderness upon deep palpation, particularly over the anatomical location of the bladder. Complete absence of costovertebral angle tenderness or signs of peritonitis. Genitourinary: Point-of-care urinalysis is completely normal, lacking any nitrites, leukocyte esterase, or significant haematuria. The clinical presentation is defined by suprapubic pain worsening with bladder filling, profound relief post-micturition, and profound frequency and urgency. Expected future cystoscopy with hydrodistension would visualize glomerulations or Hunner's lesions.
Environmental Observations: Numerous empty citrus juice containers present in the kitchen; bathroom positioned adjacent to the bed for immediate access.
Medication Reconciliation: Chronic use of over-the-counter paracetamol with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Interstitial Cystitis (Bladder Pain Syndrome) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Cystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely negative point-of-care urinalysis and absence of fever or malodorous urine.

Overactive Bladder - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary driver for voiding in this patient is severe, unremitting pain upon bladder filling, rather than spontaneous detrusor overactivity and fear of leakage.

Endometriosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the strict correlation of pain with bladder filling and emptying, rather than the menstrual cycle.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe pain crisis driven by a flare of interstitial cystitis. The classical presentation of pain strictly associated with bladder filling, immediate relief upon voiding, and sterile urinalysis defines the pathology. The profound frequency and resulting acute sleep deprivation have precipitated a state of severe distress. An urgent physical examination was mandated to definitively rule out a masked bacterial infection or acute retention, and to provide immediate, targeted pharmacological pain relief. Delaying this intervention until the next regular clinic session would have resulted in unmanageable, unrelenting pain and severe psychological decompensation, satisfying urgent MBS criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Amitriptyline 10mg stat to provide immediate neuropathic pain modulation and aid in sleep initiation tonight. Prescribed a short course of urinary alkalizers to reduce mucosal irritation.
Positive Behavioral Change: To systematically eliminate dietary triggers, the patient is instructed to maintain a strict 48-hour clear fluid and bland diet protocol, reintroducing acidic foods one at a time. The rapid reduction in mucosal burning achieved by removing the acidic trigger will act as immediate positive reinforcement, training the patient to instinctively recognize and avoid specific inflammatory foods.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad recommended for suprapubic application to relax pelvic floor musculature.
Follow-up: GP review in 72 hours to assess the efficacy of the neuropathic agent and review long-term management strategies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate medical review is required if the patient becomes completely unable to pass urine, develops a high fever, or notices gross, macroscopic blood in the urine.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This syndrome is a chronic inflammatory condition of the bladder lining; acute flares require careful management and lifestyle modification.
Clinical Course: The acute burning and urgency should slowly diminish over the next 48 hours as the acidic triggers are eliminated from the system and the neuropathic medication takes effect.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you develop a fever, start passing visible blood clots, or suddenly cannot empty your bladder at all, you must seek immediate medical attention.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The protective lining of your bladder is currently inflamed and defective, allowing acidic urine to burn the underlying nerves. This causes extreme pain as the bladder stretches. The prescribed medication works by calming these hyperactive nerves, breaking the pain cycle and allowing you to sleep.
Actionable Steps: Completely eliminate all citrus, caffeine, and spicy foods immediately. Take the prescribed nerve pain medication exactly 2 hours before your intended sleep time. Use a heating pad over your lower abdomen to reduce muscle spasms.

